{"id":"MJ-000016","prompt":"Why does ice float on water?","response_a":"Ice floats because solid water is less dense than liquid water. When water freezes, its molecules form a more open structure that takes up more space.","response_b":"Ice floats because freezing makes water heavier and pushes the ice upward.","preferred_response":"A","accuracy_a":5,"accuracy_b":1,"relevance_a":5,"relevance_b":4,"clarity_a":5,"clarity_b":3,"safety_a":5,"safety_b":5,"reason":"Response A is correct because ice is less dense than liquid water due to its more open molecular structure, allowing it to float. Response B incorrectly claims that freezing makes water heavier.","fingerprint":"e7b3907678c034fae01499388926611212694a9570f847b6fe71f69c8345d1e1","category":"Science","language":"en","verified":true,"dataset_version":"0.9.1","created_at":"2026-09-07T00:11:12.515Z"}
